# Secrets for local development — loaded automatically by `aai dev`.
# Only variables listed here are available via ctx.env (not all of process.env).
#
# For production, use `aai secret put <NAME>` to set the same keys on the server.
# In both cases, access secrets in agent code via ctx.env.MY_KEY.
#
# --- The one key the default voice pipeline needs --------------------------
#
# `aai dev` runs speech-to-text, the LLM gateway and text-to-speech on a single
# AssemblyAI key, so this is the credential a session cannot start without.
# Any ONE of these is enough, and the first two need no aai account:
#
#   1. Paste your key after the `=` below.
#   2. Or export it in your shell: export ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=<your key>
#   3. Or run `aai login`, and `aai dev` will use your account's key.
#
# Get a key at https://www.assemblyai.com/dashboard.
#
# Leaving it blank is safe: a declared-but-empty value is dropped rather than
# handed to a provider, so options 2 and 3 still work with this line as it is.
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=

# Add any other secrets your agent needs below:

# `brave_search` builtin (https://brave.com/search/api/):
BRAVE_API_KEY=

# `google_places` builtin — a key with the Places API (New) enabled:
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# `text_me` builtin and deep research reports: texts your own phone via TextBelt
# (https://textbelt.com). Buy a key there; the free key "textbelt" allows one
# text a day. SMS_TO_PHONE is your number in E.164 form, e.g. +15555550123.
TEXTBELT_KEY=
SMS_TO_PHONE=
# Other numbers a browser session's "Text me at" may name, comma-separated E.164. A
# number not listed here (or SMS_TO_PHONE) is ignored and texts go to SMS_TO_PHONE:
# the server listens on your LAN, so the field alone must not pick the recipient.
# `*` allows ANY number typed there (testing only: then anyone who can reach this
# server can make it text any number).
SMS_ALLOWED_PHONES=
# To try any of this without texting anyone, `make agent SMS=outbox`: texts are appended
# to .sms-outbox.jsonl instead, addressed to fictional numbers that override these.

# `pollen` and `air_quality` tools — use GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY above, whose
# project also needs the Pollen API and Air Quality API enabled. HOME_LOCATION is where "the pollen" means when no
# place is named: the same value as the firmware's CONFIG_AAI_DEVICE_ADDRESS
# (custom tools cannot read the device's ?location=, only the builtins can).
HOME_LOCATION=

# --- Durable workflows and uploads -----------------------------------------
#
# A DEPLOYED agent's runs are durable with no setup — the platform keeps them
# on its own database. `api.upload(file)` is the exception: an upload's record
# needs a database YOU supply, so without a DATABASE_URL the bytes live only as
# long as the sandbox even though the runs reading them do not.
#
# These four are the LOCAL equivalents, and none is needed by an agent that has
# no workflow.
#
# All three storage keys or none: two of three resolves to nothing and refuses
# by name rather than half-working. Nothing creates the bucket for you — a
# bucket is dashboard state, not a migration.
#
# From the Supabase CLI: `supabase start`, then `supabase status -o env` for
# API_URL and SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. The local stack declares one bucket, `blobs`,
# and uploads land under an `uploads/` prefix inside it — the same layout
# production uses beside its `blobs/<sha256>` deploy artifacts.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres
# AAI_UPLOAD_STORAGE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# AAI_UPLOAD_STORAGE_KEY=<SERVICE_ROLE_KEY>
# AAI_UPLOAD_STORAGE_BUCKET=blobs

# --- Local Supabase (../supabase/) ------------------------------------------
# Declared empty: `make agent` exports the real values (supabase/up.sh). The profile,
# history, reminders and calls live here, and every session start reads them.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Long-term memory: the mem0 platform (https://app.mem0.ai → API Keys) -----
# What the speaker remembers about the household is extracted and stored by mem0's
# cloud, under MEM0_USER_ID (every speaker and browser in the home shares one).
# Paste the key right after the = (no quotes). Leave MEM0_USER_ID blank for "household".
MEM0_API_KEY=
MEM0_USER_ID=

# --- Phone calls: Twilio (https://console.twilio.com) ------------------------
# The speaker places calls the household approved (tools/prepare_call.ts): the account
# SID and auth token from the console, and a Twilio number to call FROM, E.164. The call
# is answered by the calling agent (../caller, `make caller`). A trial account can only
# call numbers verified on it.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=

# --- The household's apps: Composio (https://dashboard.composio.dev → Platform) ---
# Gmail, Calendar, Slack, Notion… connected on the page's Apps panel and used by
# find_app_action / run_app_action (apps.ts). Each speaker is its own Composio user, its
# ?client= id. The project API key (ak_...) from the project's Settings → API Keys.
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=
# Signing secret of the project's webhook subscription, for triggers ("tell me when Sam
# emails"): Composio POSTs events to /api/composio/webhook, and a delivery is only trusted
# when its signature checks out. `make composio-webhook URL=https://…` sets it.
COMPOSIO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The project COMPOSIO_API_KEY belongs to (pr_..., from `composio dev projects list`), for
# `make agent`'s trigger listener. Only needed when the org has more than one project.
COMPOSIO_PROJECT_ID=
